Probably a lot less than you think, but a lot more
than you realize, too, given the concept now of
"evolutionary software" that partially
designs its own strategies for solving things.

I'd guess you'd need to encode at least a few things
that would be too hard for evolution to "guess" in a
reasonable time, though.

An AI approach, which might not be the best one at
all, would want to include in its beginning
knowledge base a few quasi-essentials like:

There is an agent "self", different from other
agents.

There is an area to explore, which is at some levels
of detail connected like a "tree", at others like a
"lattice", and at others like a "grid" with not all
cells in use, and for which in some cases agent
"self" or external circumstances (dwarf with a pick
axe, gnome with a wand of digging, rock mole with a
big appetite, vibrating square with a proper
incantation, successful search, castle drawbridge
tune) may vary the connectivity over time.

There are autonomous agents besides "self".

Some of these are friendly, some are hostile,
some are neutral, many can be any of these
varying over time and depending on both behaviors of
the "self" agent, and circumstances independent of
the "self" agent.

Some have special purposes, some are unique, some
are replicable up to specific limits, some have
particular circumstances where they are encountered.

Both short term and long term relationships can
exist, and can be encouraged or discouraged, between
agent "self" and other agents, and between other
agents.

Action at a distance is possible, action while
contiguous or juxtiposed is much more frequent;
some action at a distance is limited to the
eight cardinal directions, other has area, dispersed
ray, or similar less restricted possibilities. [Good
luck on defining "summons Kops".]

There are also "objects", which are not agents, and
which demonstrate various characteristics
appropriate to each from a limited list.

Objects can be possessed by agents.

Agent "self" can have possessions. Some of these
possessions can seem identical but not be so.

Agent "self" has a number of progressively more
convoluted ways to determine agent "self"'s current
state.

Some agents "non-self" can have and use possessions,
some cannot but may leave them behind.

Object and agents on grid cells stack, so that only
the topmost is (usually) visible. "Look more
closely, Simba." Some objects in agent "self"'s
possession list also stack, and have counts.

Various agents have possibly non-identical available
behaviors, and a limited range for each.

There are things hidden from immediate sight, and
ways to find them.

There are various immediate environments in which
agent "self" may be found, and they may differ in
style and character, so that operations that succeed
(like digging) in one environment may fail in
another for arbitrary or for determinable reasons.

There exist "devices" which are tied to specific
locations in that environment, and which may be
useful or baneful.

All places to explore that correspond to the "level"
kind of connectivity fit within a set limit in size
on a gridded graph, some come nowhere close to
filling that limit.

Agent "self" has goals, and those goals change over
time, but "survival" is always among them.

There are important subgoals on which survival
depends (staying nourished, keeping HP above 0,
keeping INT above 0 come to mind) which need
regular attention.

Most adventures don't succeed, but there are metrics
by which an attempted solution's fitness can be
gauged, including conducts (only if the solution was
successful, dying on level two by practicing conduct
"foodless" isn't a huge win for the novice 'bot),
acquisitions and their values (and maybe uniqueness)
whether successful or not, major subgoal completions
(like quest and Sokoban and Castle and Ft. Ludious),
not all of them essential, and score.

Among them are acquiring three unique objects, and
delivering one more acquired by use of them at a
particular unique spot in a particular way, to a
specific location which varies within a defined
limit.

There is a limited range of actions available, and
either a list of them, or a way to produce possible
members of that list.

Agent "self" may change in ability to perform those
actions, even within a short timescale.

Thus, agent "self" has state.

Other agents have state.

Some objects have state.

Objects and agents may be created and destroyed,
both by and independently of agent "self".

Movement at any level of detail doesn't always do
the expected thing; there are reasonable
explanations which may be gathered as to when this
might happen.

Some happenings are deterministic (e.g., shopkeeper
moving to guard the door when you owe money), some
are random (e.g., pet bumping into a monster and so
beginning a battle).

Repeating the same behavior and expecting different
results (hit a monster enough times and it *may* die,
hit a monster until your HP are gone and you *will*
die, for instance) is not a splendid first choice
of long term strategy.

Minimizing risk for "self" without going "tharn"
seems to be a highly successful early strategy
until a more complex one is evolved.

Changing alignment before the quest guarantees
failure to ascend. [This is one of the ones
that _coould_ be learned, but at great expense
of trial and error.]

"Don't go up the toplevel stairs without the
amulet!'

Once you do, "How long can you tread water?"

<grin>

The RNG hates your silicon guts! Avoid giving it
opportunities to prove that claim.

<grin>^2

There's a start for you, from an extremely larger
list, and likely these are still much too high
level. I doubt auto-rogue was what anyone today
would call "AI", at all, in that (I'm guessing)
it didn't learn to be better from run to run,
although I'd also guess it accumulated a huge
knowledge base within a single run.

It was _so_ much easier to explain this stuff and
write the 'bot when a level always had three to nine
open, rectangular rooms connected by passages that
weren't, come to the issue, very twisty at all, the
dungeon didn't branch, and all agents but "self"
had the same simple "kill the intruder" goal.

